To find $A$ and $B$, we take the following equation and multiply both sides by the common denominator:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\dfrac{x-3}{(x+4)(x+5)}&=\dfrac{A}{x+4}+\dfrac{B}{x+5}
\\\\
\displaystyle {x-3}&={A(x+5)}+{B(x+4)}
\end{align}$

Substituting $x=-4$, we get $\blueD{A=-7}$.

Substituting $x=-5$, we get $\maroonD{B=8}$.
